      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "to explore the relationship between total and sub-scores of the Pittsburgh Agitation Scale (PAS) and five proxy measures of pain in long-term care (LTC) residents."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN",
          "content": "descriptive correlational design."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SAMPLE AND SETTING",
          "content": "58 residents in three LTC facilities in rural Western Canada with moderate to severe cognitive impairment took part in the study. Six full-time registered nurses working in the facilities and three palliative care nurse consultants provided pain and agitation assessments."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
          "content": "registered nurses used PAS to assess agitation. The five proxy measures of pain were the Discomfort Scale for Dementia of the Alzheimer's Type (DS-DAT), number of pain diagnoses, use of analgesic medications, and pain ratings by both facility nurses and palliative care nurse consultants."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "there was a moderately strong relationship between total PAS agitation scores and total DS-DAT pain scores (r=0.51, P<0.01). The PAS sub-score \"resisting care\" was significantly correlated with total DS-DAT scores (r=0.46, P<0.01), and pain ratings by both facility nurses (r=0.48, P<0.01) and palliative care nurse consultants (r=0.51, P<0.01)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "for certain residents with dementia, PAS may allow assessment of both agitation and uncommunicated pain. It is possible that the PAS form of agitation \"resistance to care\" may indicate pain that individual cannot otherwise communicate. One possible response to such resistance would be to trial pain medication and reassess agitation. Nursing staff in LTC facilities may need additional training in pain assessment of residents with dementia."
        }
      ]},
